A||||13||William||Trim plates revisited once again||||1||
Z||000000||William||02-25-2000||11:54 AM||||I may have my final solution to this trim plate dilima. At Home Depot this morning, I found a 4' x 8' sheet of black laminate for 40.00. It has a textured finish, much like the black trim you see in cars, and would be enough material for about 20 screw ups! Anyone want to pitch in with ideas for cutting out the plates. I know that alot of you would like black trim plates, and this could be a low cost solution. Not sure if it would be best to cut close to the lines and then sand to the final dimeinsions or perhaps even using a laminate cutting kit that is availible for the dremel tool. I think that the gauge plate, (with the small square holes) would be the only really tough part. Thoughts?||||reg||1
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||02-25-2000||03:40 PM||||Good find William!! With that much material you should be able to come up with the best process for cutting it out. Did they have any in color? Keep us posted on the whats what in trim plates.<P>Thanks.DXR_DAD<P>PS. show us some photos as you go along.||||reg||14
Z||000002||theogre||02-25-2000||04:42 PM||||If you're doing more than one or two then make a template out of plastic or plywood. then use a router with a template collar on it. You can make hundreds of exact cuts that way. I think there is a router kit and template collars for Dremmel so you can make small holes.<P>When you make the template the openings in the template have to be larger than the finished opening. The collar is bigger than the bit by about 1/4 - 3/8" on a full size router. I'm not sure what the collar sizes are for the dremmel. I've only seen it once.<P>How thick is the sheet you are looking at. It might not hold up by itself. Are you thinking of putting it on top of the existing trim?||||reg||1
Z||000003||William||02-25-2000||04:58 PM||||The material is availible in a few colors but only black looked good (counter top laminate). Orge, The dremel router bits did not appear to be of the collar / template type. The side cutting lamination bit did have a smooth section at the bottom but I'm not sure if it would work or not. I'd rather not buy a full size router as I would have little other need for it, and hope just to use the dremel some how. Also, laminate is fairly thin and would work best glued to the old trim plates.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||02-25-2000||05:19 PM||||Not to take away anyones thunder (good idea about the laminate), but I should have a good head start on my dash cover project soon.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||02-27-2000||01:18 PM||||To have the collars for the dremmel you would need the router kit. not just bits. I remember that there was a kit, maybe a third party one, that esentially turned a dremmel into a small router. Not just the punch cutter kit that most people see in the Marts. Although making a plate and collar for the punch ring could be done fairly easy too.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||02-27-2000||01:55 PM||||The router kit for the Dremel is only about $40.  My local Wally World has them in stock.  Good idea.  I think that's what I might use for my idea/kit.||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfixit58||02-27-2000||07:43 PM||||Sears sell a tool called the Roto-zip (or something like that). IT a littler larger then a Drimel but much smaller then a router. There are all kinds of bits and attachements for as well. If your going to try to reproduce these in mass quantities, you may want something a little more powerful then the Drimel.<P>Just a thought,<BR>Roy L.<P>||||reg||3
Z||000008||87ECO5||02-27-2000||09:24 PM||||Since we're talking about dash panels ...<P>I wanted to run something by all of you.  When making a new radio surround panel, instead of making an exact copy, why not arch the top edge:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/radiosurround.jpg"> <P>I think it'd look good, it'd match the slight arch already on the gauge surround panel and it'd prevent some of us from having to attempt a super glue fiasco or purchase a new radio surround when it warps.<P>Comments?||||reg||5
Z||000009||fiero56||02-27-2000||09:56 PM||||Sounds like a pretty good idea, Scott, err, 87eco5. Are you referring to my "super glue fiasco" I had on Friday with the shift surround <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">? Well, 3 days and counting <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. I'll have to look into that. The paintball gun box is taking longer than expected. Hopefully mid-week.||||reg||1
